{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQpkgHUAV68joSRzAnGJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Settled**\n- Amounts are integer cents. VAT is 20%. Invoice totals must add up.\n- The discount is a **percentage**.\n- VAT is charged on the **discounted** subtotal, i.e. `tax = round((subtotal − discount) × 20%)`.\n\n**Mine**\n- The discount in cents will be `Math.round(subtotal × percent / 100)`, rounded once at order level.\n- The field will be called `discountPercent`. If it's missing, the discount is 0%, so existing orders behave exactly as they do now.\n\n**Questions**\n\n1. **Where does the percentage apply: to the whole order or to each line?** (You didn't answer this one.)\n   - *Order-level* (`order.discountPercent`): one rate covers everything. Because it's a percentage, each line's share follows naturally: every line is reduced by the same rate.\n   - *Per-line* (`line.discountPercent`): different lines can have different rates, e.g. a sale item next to a full-price one.\n   - **I recommend order-level.** It's what you asked for, and a percentage already spreads evenly across the lines.\n\n2. **What range of values is allowed?**\n   - *0–100, decimals allowed (e.g. 12.5); anything outside throws an error*: invalid input fails loudly, the same way `refundLine` already throws when you try to refund too many items.\n   - *Silently cap at 0–100*: nothing ever fails, but a typo like `150` would quietly make the order free.\n   - **I recommend rejecting values outside 0–100**, because silently changing a charge is worse than an error.\n\n**Held**\n- **How a line refund handles the discount.** If you pick order-level in Q1, I'll recommend refunding the line's net amount minus the order's discount rate, plus VAT on what's left. I'll also ask how to deal with per-line rounding: refunding every line separately could add up to one cent more or less than the customer actually paid. This waits on Q1."}],